 if ( false !== $timeout && $timeout < time() ) { DELETING TRANSIENT}
[...]
}}}
Otherwise transient will hang... forever. This should be tuned up so it also checks that these options exist, and delete transient if timeout is absolete.

`wp_get_nav_menu_items()` short-circuits before running its item query whenever the menu term's `count` is 0:

{{{#!php
// src/wp-includes/nav-menu.php
$args = wp_parse_args( $args, $defaults );
if ( $menu->count > 0 ) {
	$items = get_posts( $args );
} else {
	$items = array();
}
}}}

`$menu->count` is not a safe proxy for "no items match these args":

 * '''It counts published items only.''' `_update_post_term_count()` counts posts with `post_status = 'publish'` (the default of the `update_post_term_count_statuses` filter), so a menu whose items are all `draft` or `trash` reports `count = 0` even though a caller querying `'draft'`, `'any'`, `'publish,draft'`, or `'trash'` has matching items.
 * '''It can be stale.''' `wp_defer_term_counting( true )` leaves the count at 0 while items are already attached, and `wp_get_nav_menu_object()` returns a passed `WP_Term` object verbatim, so a caller holding a term snapshot taken before items were added is gated as well.

`post_status` is a documented argument of `wp_get_nav_menu_items()`, so all of the following return `array()` while `get_posts()` with the same arguments returns real items:

 * a draft-only menu queried with `post_status` `'draft'`, `'any'`, or `'publish,draft'`;
 * a trash-only menu queried with `'trash'` (trashing the last published item returns the count to 0);
 * a published item attached while term counting is deferred — hidden even from the default `'publish'` query;
 * any item hidden behind a stale `WP_Term` snapshot passed as `$menu`.

== In-core consequences ==

 * '''Colliding `menu_order`.''' `wp_update_nav_menu_item()` positions a new item by querying `'publish,draft'` items and using `1 +` the last item's `menu_order` (`src/wp-includes/nav-menu.php`, ~line 464). On a menu with no published items — e.g. one being assembled as drafts — the gate empties the list, so the new item gets `menu_order = 0`; every subsequent item collides at position 0 instead of being appended.
 * '''Duplicate auto-added pages.''' `_wp_auto_add_pages_to_menu()` queries the `'publish,draft'` item list to skip pages already in the menu (~line 1164). On a menu with no published items the check sees an empty list and inserts a second item for the same page.

Other in-core callers requesting non-default statuses are affected the same way: `wp_get_nav_menu_to_edit()` queries `'any'` and `wp_nav_menu_update_menu_items()` queries `'draft,publish'` (`src/wp-admin/includes/nav-menu.php`, ~lines 1266 and 1409), so a draft-only menu lists empty on the edit screen and its items can't be updated or deleted there — the save path can't see them either.

== Steps to reproduce ==

{{{#!php
$menu_id = wp_create_nav_menu( 'example' );
$menu    = wp_get_nav_menu_object( $menu_id );

$item_id = wp_update_nav_menu_item(
	$menu_id,
	0,
	array(
		'menu-item-type'   => 'custom',
		'menu-item-title'  => 'Example',
		'menu-item-url'    => 'https://example.org/',
		'menu-item-status' => 'draft',
	)
);

// Returns array() — the gate short-circuits because $menu->count is 0 (drafts are not counted):
$via_function = wp_get_nav_menu_items( $menu_id, array( 'post_status' => 'draft' ) );

// Returns array( $item_id ) — the underlying query does find the item:
$via_query = wp_list_pluck(
	get_posts(
		array(
			'post_type'   => 'nav_menu_item',
			'post_status' => 'draft',
			'nopaging'    => true,
			'tax_query'   => array(
				array(
					'taxonomy' => 'nav_menu',
					'field'    => 'term_taxonomy_id',
					'terms'    => $menu->term_taxonomy_id,
				),
			),
		)
	),
	'ID'
);
}}}

== Root cause ==

The gate was introduced in [53010] (git `556492ad88`) for #55372, which replaced `get_objects_in_term()` + `$args['include']` with a `tax_query` to save one query. The previous `! empty( $items )` check tested whether any objects were attached to the term at all — it was status-agnostic and reflected actual attachments. The replacement `$menu->count > 0` tests the published count only and can additionally be stale, so the gate now fires in cases the old check never hit.

== Proposed fix ==

Remove the short-circuit and always run `get_posts( $args )` — restoring the pre-[53010] approach of letting the query, not the term count, decide whether matching items exist. `get_posts()` already returns `array()` for a genuinely empty menu.

A narrower alternative — keeping the gate only for the default `post_status === 'publish'` query — was considered and rejected: deferred term counting and stale `WP_Term` snapshots both leave `count = 0` while published items exist, so a publish-only gate would still return a wrong `array()` in precisely the cases this ticket covers.

Performance impact of removing the gate (measured in a review sweep of the attached patch):

 * Warm caches, and sites with a persistent object cache: '''zero additional queries''' after a one-time cache miss per menu — the query result, including the empty result, is cached in the `post-queries` group. (That result cache did not yet exist when the gate was added in 6.0.)
 * A site with no object cache: one additional indexed `SELECT` per empty rendered menu per request — it joins `term_relationships` and filters on `term_taxonomy_id` plus the `type_status_date` index, returning 0 rows.
 * Non-empty menus: identical — the gate already passed through to the same query.

== Tests ==

The patch adds 7 regression cases to `Tests_Post_Nav_Menu`: draft-only menus queried with `'draft'`, `'any'`, and `'publish,draft'`; `menu_order` positioning of a second draft item; deferred term counting; a stale `WP_Term` snapshot; and a trash-only menu queried with `'trash'`. Each fails before the change and passes after.

In the same review sweep, 762 existing tests across the menu, customize, REST menus, ajax nav-menus, and related groups passed with the change applied, and the `_wp_auto_add_pages_to_menu()` duplicate insert described above was reproduced under the old code and shown to be gone after it.

== Notes ==

 * A GitHub pull request for code review will be linked shortly.
 * AI assistance was used in preparing the patch and is disclosed in the PR per the [https://make.wordpress.org/ai/handbook/ai-guidelines/ WordPress AI guidelines].

The feed shouldn't use only the 'post_modified_gmt' to define the lastBuildDate it does not always correspond to the date to be used.
So, on the file wp-includes/feed.php
Instead of 
{{{#!php
<?php
	// Extract the post modified times from the posts.
	$modified_times = wp_list_pluck( $wp_query->posts, 'post_modified_gmt' );
}}}
Should be
{{{#!php
<?php
	// Extract the post modified times from the posts.
	$modified_times = wp_list_pluck( $wp_query->posts, 'post_modified_gmt' );
	$modified_times = array_merge( $modified_times, wp_list_pluck( $wp_query->posts, 'post_date_gmt' ) );
}}}

this is causing the feed to not update the lastBuildDate when you schedule the post.

== Summary:

Code in wp-include/blocks.php pulls out the option on line 1708:
{{{
$sticky = get_option( 'sticky_posts' );
}}}

Then assumes it is an array, when passing it into array_merge on line 1720:
{{{
$query['post__not_in'] = array_merge( $query['post__not_in'], $sticky );
}}}

This can cause a fatal error if the option is not set to an array:
{{{
Fatal error: Uncaught TypeError: array_merge(): Argument #2 must be of type array, string given 
}}}

Other parts of the code are defensive about the data coming from {{{ get_option( 'sticky_posts' ); }}} , but this part is not. 

== Steps to Reproduce:

* Use WP 6.4.2 and theme Twenty Twenty-Four Version: 1.0 
* Set your sticky_posts option to an empty string (an unexpected type)

{{{
MariaDB [wordpress]> select * from wp_options where option_name='sticky_posts';
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
| option_id | option_name  | option_value | autoload |
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
|        75 | sticky_posts | a:0:{}       | yes      |
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
1 row in set (0.000 sec)

MariaDB [wordpress]> update wp_options set option_value='' where option_id=75;
Query OK, 1 row affected (0.012 sec)
Rows matched: 1  Changed: 1  Warnings: 0

MariaDB [wordpress]> select * from wp_options where option_name='sticky_posts';
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
| option_id | option_name  | option_value | autoload |
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
|        75 | sticky_posts |              | yes      |
+-----------+--------------+--------------+----------+
1 row in set (0.000 sec)
}}}

* Go to site editor, patterns, "List of posts without images, 1 column" and duplicate it
* Edit the duplicate
* Navigate to the query loop block
* On the right sidebar, turn off "Inherit query from template"
* Change "Sticky Posts" from "Include" to "Exclude"
* Attempt to save
* A fatal error is triggered
* Changing the "Sticky Posts" back to "Include" or changing the option value to a serialized array allows saving again

On the home page of the theme the wording for when the query block has no posts is referring to search results and prompting you to choose different keywords. This doesn't make sense outside of the search results template and it's confusing for users. We should change to a more generic text or simply duplicate the pattern for both use cases


To test, go to the Site Editor and select the Blog home template, scroll all the way to the footer and you'll see the text:


